<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Show&nbsp;replies&nbsp;to&nbsp;questions</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>What is happening over there?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Business as usual.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Can the Foundation contact them?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This dimension's Foundation cannot.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Are they trying to contact our universe's Foundation?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No. The butterfly was never supposed to end up in this dimension.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What was the message they were trying to send?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Something that would possibly alter this dimension's timestream if it was passed on to the wrong Mark Kiryu.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>My initial take on this was that it's inherently not that big of a deal to see these light butterflies flying around in other dimensions, but they're not supposed to exist at all in the current dimension, at least not for a hundred years or whatever. The Foundation wanted something fast (hence the light) and smart, to a degree (hence the nerve cells and the passage of words directly from one iteration of a Foundation staff member to another).</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Potential&nbsp;spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>This dimension's Foundation finding it was entirely a mistake. Two messenger butterflies got smashed together en route to the same place, crossed the streams, quantum entanglement, what have you. Admittedly, I'm not sure if that got through in the interview, but I wanted to leave enough room for the reader to put together their own comprehension of the little dimension hopper messenger butterfly.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

